# Two terminals, two colours — the rail tells them apart.
#
# Every pane wears a one-cell rail down its left edge in the colour it
# took when it opened, off the ladder the SESSIONS panel draws from
# (`ui/pane_rail.zig`). The colour is the point: before this, two
# ghostty panes side by side were two identical grey rectangles.
#
# The ladder hands out its slots in order and skips what is already
# worn, so the first pane is onedark's green #98c379 and the second its
# blue #61afef — never the same colour while both are open. The colour
# is the only way a script can see the rail at all: the glyph is the
# same ▌ on both.
#
# `sleep 30` keeps each child alive; an exited pane paints its banner
# over the pane and the rail is all that is left of it.
command view.toggle_tree
ex term printf LL; sleep 30
expect screen contains "LL"
ex term printf RR; sleep 30
expect screen contains "RR"

# The rail is the first column of each pane, in that pane's colour.
expect color 0 2 fg #98c379
expect color 0 21 fg #61afef
# And the two are not each other's.
expect color 0 2 fg not #61afef
expect color 0 21 fg not #98c379
# It runs the pane's full height, not just its first row.
expect color 0 19 fg #98c379
